\begin{abstract}
This manuscript introduces DF-HITSE, a dual-face hydro-informatics and telemetry simulation 
engine integrating incompressible fluid transmission, Hamiltonian mechanics, symplectic 
integration, and cryptographic synchronization. We derive the port-Hamiltonian operator 
governing hydraulic propagation, prove global boundedness via Lyapunov energy methods, and 
establish symplectic invariance for long-duration stability. A crypto-hydraulic coupling 
theorem links pressure gradients to post-quantum key exchange throughput. Hardware-in-the-loop 
validation confirms theoretical predictions, demonstrating zero packet loss at 1.2 MHz 
telemetry sampling and deterministic zeroization under turbulence onset. DF-HITSE forms a 
multi-scale engine family with TSSE and AST-TSDME, establishing a unified computational 
framework for fluid dynamics, telemetry, and secure communication.
\end{abstract}